{"title":"Cahn–Hilliard spinodal decomposition","markdown":{"headingText":"Cahn–Hilliard spinodal decomposition","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-cahn-hilliard","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n`apps/cahn_hilliard` — conserved fourth-order phase separation, parameterised\nfor the Fe–Cr miscibility gap.\n\n## Physical setting\n\nAn Fe–Cr alloy held at 475 °C inside its chemical spinodal separates\nspontaneously into Fe-rich and Cr-rich domains. There is no nucleation barrier:\nevery long-wavelength composition fluctuation grows. The technological interest\nis that this is the mechanism behind \"475 °C embrittlement\" in ferritic\nstainless steels, and the length scale of the resulting microstructure sets the\nmechanical consequence.\n\n## Governing equations\n\nOne conserved composition field $c$ (Cr mole fraction):\n\n$$\n\\partial_t c \\;=\\; M\\nabla^2\\!\\left( f'(c) - \\kappa\\nabla^2 c \\right),\n$$ {#eq-ch-pde}\n\nwith the regular-solution bulk free energy in units of $RT$,\n\n$$\nf(c) \\;=\\; \\omega\\,c(1-c) \\;+\\; c\\ln c \\;+\\; (1-c)\\ln(1-c),\n\\qquad \\omega = \\frac{\\Omega}{RT}.\n$$ {#eq-ch-f}\n\nThe defaults $T = 748.15\\,\\mathrm{K}$ and\n$\\Omega = 20.1\\,\\mathrm{kJ\\,mol^{-1}}$ place Fe–32Cr inside the spinodal. This\nis a documented representative miscibility-gap model, not a CALPHAD\nassessment, and the report does not claim quantitative agreement with a\nthermodynamic database.\n\n## Spectral formulation\n\nLinearising the bulk term about the alloy composition $c_0$ puts $f''(c_0)$\ninto the linear symbol and leaves the remainder to the nonlinearity. With\n$u = k_{\\mathrm{lap}}$,\n\n$$\nL(k) \\;=\\; M\\big( f''(c_0)\\,u \\;-\\; \\kappa\\,u^2 \\big),\n\\qquad\nM_{\\mathrm{nl}}(k) \\;=\\; M\\,u ,\n$$ {#eq-ch-symbol}\n\n$$\nN(c) \\;=\\; f'(c) - f'(c_0) - f''(c_0)\\,(c-c_0).\n$$ {#eq-ch-nl}\n\nBoth channels carry the factor $u$, so the mean composition is conserved\nexactly.\n\nThe growth rate follows directly from @eq-ch-symbol. Writing\n$|\\mathbf{k}| = k$,\n\n$$\n\\lambda(k) \\;=\\; -M k^2\\big( f''(c_0) + \\kappa k^2 \\big),\n$$ {#eq-ch-growth}\n\nwhich is positive — unstable — precisely when $f''(c_0) < 0$, the spinodal\ncondition. The fastest-growing mode is at\n$k_{\\max}^2 = -f''(c_0)/(2\\kappa)$, and this wavelength is what sets the\ninitial domain spacing.\n\nThe nonlinearity @eq-ch-nl is not a low-order polynomial — it contains\n$\\ln\\!\\big(c/(1-c)\\big)$ — so dealiasing is on.\n\n## Solution strategy\n\nETD1 as in @eq-etd1. Composition is clamped away from $\\{0,1\\}$ so the\nlogarithms stay finite; a sampled composition outside the physical range stops\nthe run rather than producing quiet nonsense.\n\nOptional collective CSV diagnostics record mass, composition bounds, and the\ncurrent-state total energy including the spectral gradient contribution.\n\n## Verification\n\n* **Analytical growth rate.** A single seeded mode grows as\n  $e^{\\lambda(k)t}$ with $\\lambda$ from @eq-ch-growth, matched within 5%.\n* **Mass conservation.** The mean composition is held to $10^{-12}$ across a\n  run.\n* **Free energy decreases.** The total free energy falls monotonically while a\n  spinodal mode grows.\n* **Gradient energy.** The diagnostic gradient energy matches the analytical\n  value on distributed grids.\n* **Decomposition independence.** Seeded noise produces the same field and the\n  same mean on any number of ranks.\n\n## Binaries and inputs\n\n`cahn_hilliard` (CPU), `cahn_hilliard_hip`. Presets: `fe_cr_spinodal.json`\n(single-mode verification) and `coarsening.json` (seeded broadband\ncoarsening with diagnostics).\n\n## Scalability\n\nNot measured. Shares the spectral ETD stack with @sec-tungsten.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"e